# DNA-AND content · life · viruses
# researched: Wikipedia "Baltimore classification", "Virus classification"; standard virology for
#   capsid shapes, envelopes and the lytic and lysogenic cycles
# Viruses are not cells; they sit outside the three domains. Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r08-life-sim.md

# ── Baltimore groups (how a virus's genes get read) ──

@virusgroup group-1
name: Double-stranded DNA viruses (group I)
real: yes
example: herpes viruses, pox viruses, many bacteriophages

@virusgroup group-2
name: Single-stranded DNA viruses (group II)
real: yes
example: parvoviruses

@virusgroup group-3
name: Double-stranded RNA viruses (group III)
real: yes
example: rotavirus

@virusgroup group-4
name: Positive-sense RNA viruses (group IV)
real: yes
says: their RNA can be read straight away like a message
example: cold viruses, coronaviruses, polio

@virusgroup group-5
name: Negative-sense RNA viruses (group V)
real: yes
says: their RNA must be copied before it can be read
example: flu, measles, rabies, Ebola

@virusgroup group-6
name: RNA retroviruses (group VI)
real: yes
says: write their RNA back into DNA and splice it into the host's own genes
example: HIV

@virusgroup group-7
name: DNA reverse-transcribing viruses (group VII)
real: yes
example: hepatitis B

# ── Shapes and parts ──

@part capsid
name: Capsid
real: yes
says: the protein shell around the virus's genes

@part envelope
name: Envelope
real: yes
says: a fatty outer skin stolen from the last cell it left; some viruses have one, some are naked

@part spikes
name: Spikes
real: yes
says: proteins on the surface that latch onto a host cell

@shape helical
name: Helical virus
real: yes
says: a long spiral rod
example: tobacco mosaic virus, rabies

@shape icosahedral
name: Twenty-sided virus
real: yes
says: a round 20-faced shell
example: herpes, polio

@shape complex
name: Complex virus
real: yes
says: a head, a tail and legs, or other odd shapes
example: T4 bacteriophage, pox viruses

@shape giant
name: Giant virus
real: yes
says: viruses bigger than some bacteria, with huge genomes
example: mimivirus

# ── Who they infect ──

@host animal-viruses
name: Animal viruses
real: yes

@host plant-viruses
name: Plant viruses
real: yes
example: mosaic viruses

@host bacteriophages
name: Bacteriophages
real: yes
says: viruses that hunt bacteria; the most common biological things on Earth

@host archaea-viruses
name: Archaea viruses
real: yes

@host fungus-viruses
name: Fungus viruses
real: yes

# ── Life cycles ──

@cycle lytic
name: Lytic cycle
real: yes
says: inject, copy, burst the cell open, spread

@cycle lysogenic
name: Lysogenic cycle
real: yes
says: hide inside the host's DNA, copied along with it, until something wakes the virus up

@cycle latent
name: Latency
real: yes
says: lie dormant in the body for years, then flare up
example: herpes, chickenpox becoming shingles

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule virus-not-alive
real: rule
says: viruses have no cells and no metabolism; they only copy themselves inside a host

@rule virus-chemistry-ai
real: rule
says: viruses run on the chemistry layer of the mini AI only: latch on, inject, copy, burst

@rule virus-mutation
real: rule
says: RNA viruses mutate fastest; viruses jump between species when they mutate to fit a new host's cells

@rule virus-gene-carry
real: rule
says: viruses can carry genes from one species into another (Viral Gene Carry power, and natural events)

@rule virus-host-range
real: rule
says: a virus can only infect hosts whose cells its spikes can latch onto

@rule virus-plagues
real: rule
says: dense populations let viruses spread fast; immunity, isolation and medicine slow them
